      "section": "time.clock",
      "confidence": "low",
      "note": "Aymara has no past/to system; used the additive '<hour> ura <minutes> minutuni' form (ura = hour, minutu = minute, -ni = 'with', mirroring 'tunka mayani'). :30 uses 'chikani' (chika = half). Minute numerals: phisqa, tunka, tunka phisqani, pä tunka, kimsa tunka, pusi tunka, phisqa tunka. Standard 12-hour count (no rotated hour list). The -ni compounding on 'minutu' is plausible but unverified by a native speaker. Specific doubts: (1) whether '-ni' attaches to 'minutu' in spoken time or speakers just juxtapose '{h} ura <n> minutu'; (2) whether 'chikani' or 'chika' is used for half past; (3) whether the hour phrase takes the declarative '-wa' ('kimsa urawa') even in isolation."
